\subsection{What the row cannot express}\label{sec:cannot}

\begin{enumerate}\setlength\itemsep{0pt}
\item \textbf{Total-degree caps.} The 2-D cap tables and the $N$-D \code{joint} cap keep a subset of the tensor pairs (the last columns of Tables~\ref{tab:2d} and \ref{tab:nd}): the light $L_2[x,y]$ block keeps, of the $16$ pairs at lengths $2$, the seven with no monomial mixing the two directions; heavy keeps nine, dropping $x\theta_x$ and $y\theta_y$ instead and keeping the mixed pairs, which is why neither contains the other; the $N$-D cap keeps the pairs of total degree at most $2$ over $2N$ variables. The row has no cap. The measured record on caps: in the 1-D $H_\infty$ gain the stock \code{joint} cap $3$ cost nothing in the $Q$ form and $4\times10^{-3}$ in the coercive form (lift notes, Sec.~7), and in 2-D the uncapped faces at the weight degree cost $10^6$ variables where the capped stock sizing is affordable (lift notes, heat benchmark, 10/08/2026).
\item \textbf{The 2-D \code{sep} ties.} The stock 2-D block keeps four basis operators, lower and upper in each direction, and equates the four kernels of the result; the row has one complete-moment basis operator. A Gram on the single operator is a Gram on the four with equal blocks, so the single-basis family is contained in the tied family; the reverse inclusion has not been established.
\item \textbf{The 1-D spaces of a 2-D PIE.} The 2-D tiers give a 1-D space the complete moment alone ($d_1=0$, Table~\ref{tab:2d}) where the 1-D tiers give it both moments ($d_1=2$, Table~\ref{tab:1d}). The row, indexed by the number of variables of the space, holds one of the two. The routing record of 11/13/2026 measured the 1-D entry on the 1-D spaces of a 2-D PIE: the edge cascades S3 and S8 certified at $0.9\,r^*$ with no raise where the stock block returned UNKNOWN after one.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Measurements before a change of defaults}\label{sec:measure}

The row is a different family from every stock block in two or more variables, so no default moves without the comparison below, at equal lengths and at equal size, with the stock block as the reference. Each item names its record.
\begin{enumerate}\setlength\itemsep{0pt}
\item The \code{fin2} gain ladder (\file{settings_sopvar/README.md}; exact $0.068606888$): stock gaps $5.0\times10^{-3}$, $4.7\times10^{-4}$, $3.7\times10^{-5}$, $2.4\times10^{-5}$ at stripped, light, heavy, veryheavy, with $107$k, $192$k, $759$k, $2.39$M variables. The per-space defaults gave $4.8\times10^{-4}$ at every tier above stripped.
\item The 2-D $H_\infty$ estimator of library PDE~48: stock light $0.0061794$ at $196$k variables; the best bound measured $0.0024154$ at $2.41$M variables (per-space defaults with \code{mult} 1, \code{int} 2, \code{joint} 4).
\item Library 2-D rows 33--35, 37, 39, 40 (stability, form \code{'P'}) and 45--47 (gains): identical outcomes and sizes within $0.1\%$ under the per-space defaults.
\item The mixed-domain suite, \code{test\_mixed\_domain\_pde('exec')}: $41$ checks, of which $28$ pass under either storage; the five $H_\infty$ gains at light and heavy (gaps $1.2\times10^{-4}$ to $3.7\times10^{-3}$); S3 and S8 at $0.9\,r^*$.
\item Three variables: the heat stability and gain rows of \code{heatNd} (light certified in $289$ s; the gain $41\%$ above exact at light), under the $N$-D row with and without the \code{joint} cap, and with \code{uniform} against \code{all} patterns where the SDP solves.
\item The 1-D library baselines at light and heavy, which Proposition~\ref{prop:span} predicts unchanged; a bit-identical program is the check.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Open decisions}\label{sec:open}

\begin{enumerate}\setlength\itemsep{0pt}
\item \textbf{Offsets per direction.} Rule \eqref{eq:d4} lowers the length of a term in the directions of its factors; the builder lowers every direction. The rule keeps every term within the degree of the plain term and no smaller; the measurement is the cost and the bound of the faces in 2-D and 3-D under each.
\item \textbf{Faces at $d_3=2$.} The slack rule \code{'auto'} keeps the faces at the plain length when any weight degree is $1$, so that no face carries a constant multiplier; rule \eqref{eq:d4} gives them length $1$ there. Which to make the default is a measurement on the 2-D heat benchmark, where the faces were needed.
\item \textbf{Caps.} Three options: no cap, the row as stated; an optional total-degree cap per block, one number, the \code{joint} field of the code, which writes the $N$-D tiers and not the 2-D ones; or the 2-D cap tables kept as an override. The first two are a measurement of item~1 of Section~\ref{sec:measure}; the third is the present state.
\item \textbf{Patterns in two or more variables.} \code{uniform} is the default of every present tier; \code{all} is the form of the conjecture, at the cost of Table~\ref{tab:size}. The named sets suffice until a PIE is found that \code{uniform} fails and a strict subset of \code{all} certifies.
\item \textbf{The 1-D spaces of an $N$-D PIE.} Item~3 of Section~\ref{sec:cannot}: the 1-D entry, measured once in favour on S3 and S8, or the complete moment alone, as the 2-D tiers have it.
\item \textbf{Names.} The field names of the layout are provisional; the length convention in each name is not.
\item \textbf{Citations.} Karlin and Studden (1966) for the scalar Markov--Luk\'acs theorem, Dette and Studden (2002) for the matrix case, Schm\"udgen (1991), Putinar (1993) and Scherer and Hol (2006) are cited from memory and are to be verified before any of this enters the manual.
\item \textbf{The conjecture.} Unproven; the Poincar\'e case shows that the least $d_1$ is not read from the support, so no rule of Section~\ref{sec:settings} is complete. The role of the row is to make every certificate the code can build nameable in the factors of \eqref{eq:cert}, not to decide which one exists.
\end{enumerate}

